# Copy to `.env` beside this file and edit. `docker compose` reads
# `.env` from its own directory automatically, so run it from `docker/`:
#
#   cd docker
#   cp .env.example .env
#   $EDITOR .env
#   mkdir -p "$CHITRAGUPTA_WORKSPACE" "$CHITRAGUPTA_WORKSPACE/papers"
#   docker compose up -d
#
# `.env` is gitignored. This file is not: it is the documentation of
# what the compose file reads, so keep the two in step.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------

# Which service starts. Required in one form or another -- neither
# service in docker-compose.yml is enabled without a profile, so a plain
# `docker compose up` with this unset starts nothing at all and says so.
#
#   cpu  no GPU handed to the container. Works everywhere. The enrich
#        stages run on the CPU torch wheel, just slower.
#   gpu  every GPU on the host. Needs the NVIDIA Container Toolkit
#        installed outside the container; without it, `up` fails with
#        "could not select device driver" rather than falling back.
#
# Overridable per command: `docker compose --profile gpu up -d`.
COMPOSE_PROFILES=cpu

# REQUIRED. Host directory mounted at /workspace -- where `chitragupta
# init` scaffolds and where drafts land. No default: compose refuses to
# start rather than mounting something arbitrary.
#
# Create it, and `papers/` inside it, before the first `up`. The Docker
# daemon creates a missing bind-mount source itself as root:root, and
# for the read-only papers mount that yields a directory nobody can add
# a PDF to -- read-only inside the container, root-owned outside it,
# silently.
CHITRAGUPTA_WORKSPACE=/absolute/path/to/your/workspace

# Host directory of PDFs, mounted READ-ONLY at /workspace/papers.
# Defaults to $CHITRAGUPTA_WORKSPACE/papers. Point it at a shared corpus
# to use one set of PDFs from several workspaces.
#CHITRAGUPTA_PAPERS=/absolute/path/to/papers

# Host directory for the agent's ~/.claude. This is what persists the
# login, so a restart is not a re-authentication. Relative paths are
# resolved against docker/.
#CHITRAGUPTA_CLAUDE_HOME=./claude

# The container's name: what `docker exec -it <name> bash` takes. Also
# becomes the container's hostname and the agent's name in the
# remote-control UI, so make it recognisable if you run more than one.
#CHITRAGUPTA_CONTAINER_NAME=chitragupta-claude

# Compose's project name, which prefixes the network and any named
# volume. Set it per workspace to run two of these side by side --
# together with a distinct CHITRAGUPTA_CONTAINER_NAME, which is the one
# that would actually collide.
#CHITRAGUPTA_PROJECT_NAME=chitragupta

# Which image tag to run. Build a candidate as `:tmp` and point one
# workspace at it without disturbing the `:latest` everything else uses.
#CHITRAGUPTA_IMAGE_TAG=latest

# REQUIRED. The unprivileged account inside the image, and its numeric
# ids. There is deliberately **no default** for any of the three, in the
# compose file or the Dockerfile: this account is a property of your
# machine, not of the project, and a default that is wrong for almost
# everyone is worse than none -- it works just well enough to write
# files owned by the wrong uid into your workspace, which you then
# cannot edit from outside the container.
#
# Fill these in from your own account:
#
#     echo "CHITRAGUPTA_USER=$(id -un)"
#     echo "CHITRAGUPTA_UID=$(id -u)"
#     echo "CHITRAGUPTA_GID=$(id -g)"
#
# One value to avoid: uid **1000** is already taken inside the image by
# the Node base image's own `node` account, and two accounts sharing a
# numeric owner on a bind mount is its own kind of confusing.
#
# These are build args as well as runtime settings, so changing them
# needs `docker compose build`, not just a restart.
CHITRAGUPTA_USER=your-login-name
CHITRAGUPTA_UID=1001
CHITRAGUPTA_GID=1001
